│ patch_name               │ repair_exchange_saturation                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ This approach keeps the input graph as the starting state, repairs it by deleting edges   │
│                          │ that participate in the largest number of current common-neighbor violations, then        │
│                          │ performs degree-balanced C4-safe saturation.                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ After obtaining a maximal C4-free graph, it applies several small edge-exchange           │
│                          │ perturbations: delete a few strategically chosen high-degree edges, greedily refill using │
│                          │ randomized balanced ordering, and retain the best valid result. Every candidate is        │
│                          │ explicitly repaired/verified using the common-neighbor characterization before it can be  │
│                          │ returned.                                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Unlike the original one-pass repair and fixed lexicographic augmentation, this uses       │
│                          │ global violation scores and local search around the input-derived graph, which should     │
│                          │ provide more consistent improvements across different input matrices.                     │
