│ patch_name               │ repair_refill_search                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ This algorithm treats the supplied matrix as a genuine starting state rather than         │
│                          │ replacing it with a fixed construction. It first symmetrizes and repairs the input using  │
│                          │ targeted deletions: edges participating in the largest number of common-neighbor          │
│                          │ violations are removed first. This preserves more useful structure than deleting          │
│                          │ arbitrary edges.                                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ After repair, it repeatedly adds only edges with no existing length-three path between    │
│                          │ their endpoints. That condition is exactly the safe edge-addition criterion for           │
│                          │ maintaining C4-freeness. Additions are selected with a degree-balancing score, favoring   │
│                          │ lower-degree endpoints and lower estimated blocking cost.                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Several randomized repair trajectories are tried from the original input. The best        │
│                          │ saturated result is then improved with bounded one- and two-edge perturbations: remove    │
│                          │ structurally expensive edges, greedily refill, and retain only strictly better C4-free    │
│                          │ graphs. Every repair and refill stage explicitly checks the common-neighbor invariant.    │
