│ patch_name               │ adaptive_pathswap_beam                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ This version keeps the input-derived scaffold philosophy of the prior programs, but       │
│                          │ changes the local-search emphasis from broad percentile-based kicks to adaptive           │
│                          │ path-unlocking swaps.                                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The main ideas are:                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ - Start from several different C4-free repairs of actual input edges, using               │
│                          │ source-degree, source-centrality, balance, and randomized orderings.                      │
│                          │ - Saturate each scaffold with a legality-preserving greedy process. Candidate insertions  │
│                          │ are scored using both estimated future legal-edge destruction and degree-balancing        │
│                          │ pressure.                                                                                 │
│                          │ - Maintain a compact archive of distinct valid states.                                    │
│                          │ - Use a new path-unlocking ruin/refill phase: missing edges blocked by a unique           │
│                          │ length-three path identify specific existing edges whose deletion can unlock              │
│                          │ replacements. Edge deletion scores combine those unlock credits, endpoint degree, and     │
│                          │ local imbalance.                                                                          │
│                          │ - Try both single-edge and multi-edge removals. Removed edges are temporarily banned      │
│                          │ during refill, encouraging genuinely new replacements rather than simply restoring the    │
│                          │ deleted edge.                                                                             │
│                          │ - Explicitly validate the “at most one common neighbor” invariant after every scaffold,   │
│                          │ refill, swap candidate, and final repair.                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The algorithm remains input-dependent throughout: all initial states are constructed from │
│                          │ the supplied adjacency matrix, while later additions and deletions directly edit those    │
│                          │ states rather than returning a fixed graph construction.                                  │
